WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
The titan began its nearly 13,000-foot descent&nbsp;
but the vessel and its five person crew vanished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.640
in less than two hours. This is not the first&nbsp;
time a mission to the "Titanic" shipwreck has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:19.860
gone awry. 20 years ago science editor was&nbsp;
sent on assignment all the way down to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:27.840
"Titanic" in a submersible when he got stuck.
&gt;&gt; I felt a little bit of a boom, didn't you?&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.240
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Oh, my gosh.&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:35.100
&gt;&gt; Look at the side.
&gt;&gt; Are we stuck or what?&nbsp;

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:41.640
&gt;&gt; Reporter: We appear to be wedged&nbsp;
alongside the propeller beneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.140
the wreck of the upside-down stern.
&gt;&gt; Luckily there was a three-person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:51.919
crew and they were able to break free, a&nbsp;
moment captured and camera. Watch this. &gt;&gt;

00:00:51.919 --> 00:01:00.780
&gt;&gt; Reporter: After half an hour Victor's piloting&nbsp;
skills and cool-headed attitude win the day. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.020
out?
&gt;&gt;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:10.560
Joining us now is the scientist, the journalist&nbsp;
and author. Thank you for being with us. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:18.780
I read your account I was so shocked because you&nbsp;
don't even like going under the water at all. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:25.140
so when you got this offer as a journalist at ABC&nbsp;
news you felt you had to take it because of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.820
you felt you would see, and then you get stuck in&nbsp;
the propeller. You have a perspective almost no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:36.900
one on Earth has of what it is like to go down.&nbsp;
Can you tell us about that experience given what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:45.480
we're going through looking for this right now?
&gt;&gt; Yes. Good morning. That's right. I have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:49.980
deathly fear of water. When I was invited&nbsp;
to be the first TV correspondent to report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.462
from the "Titanic," my impulse was, oh, god,&nbsp;
no. Of all things I don't want to do that.

00:01:54.462 --> 00:01:54.474
[6:33:11 AM]

00:01:54.474 --> 00:02:00.600
that. But then I had to do my job. When we got&nbsp;
stuck down there, two and a half miles beneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:09.120
the surface, it was the sense of being buried&nbsp;
alive under that much water. And I can't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:16.740
begin to tell you how terrifying that is because&nbsp;
when we first collided, there was just a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:22.200
of orientation. When you're driving and it's a&nbsp;
beautiful, sunny day, on top of the world, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:26.880
thinking everything is going great. And then&nbsp;
suddenly, some other car comes out of nowhere and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:33.600
slams into you. There's that moment of confusion&nbsp;
what just happened. Once we realized this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:41.100
a serious thing, the sub felt silent because&nbsp;
neither I or my driving buddy wanted to disturb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:47.460
the pilot on the edge of his seat monitoring the&nbsp;
situation, speaking in Russian to the mothership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:53.520
above us. We thought, wow, how are we going to&nbsp;
get out of this? My scientific brain started

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.780
My scientific brain started ticking&nbsp;
off all the ways we could get out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:06.180
You realize there is no way out. You're in the&nbsp;
middle of the north atlantic at the bottom. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:12.540
can't call aaa to tow you out. I experienced&nbsp;
enormous sadness. The only way to describe it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.320
as if the weight of the ocean came down on me. I&nbsp;
thought of my wife, we were newlyweds. It was my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:32.160
anniversary. I ultimately experienced a sense&nbsp;
of peace. All three these 72 hours thinking of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:38.280
these souls down there and I know what they're&nbsp;
experiencing. I wonder if they're still alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:45.300
We're hearing noises, but we can't tell what&nbsp;
those noises are. We're past the point where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:59.016
they're running out of oxygen. Noise travels&nbsp;
very well in water, much better than in the

00:03:59.016 --> 00:04:01.560
water, much better than in the air,&nbsp;
and we've been listening for it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:10.020
It might just be a piece of metal or a part of the&nbsp;
ship banging together. We may never know. We may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:14.940
never recover this vessel.
&gt;&gt; Let me ask,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:20.760
what are you -- a lot of people point to the&nbsp;
people on the submersible and said some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.020
them have a lot of experience. They've&nbsp;
done -- they would know what to do in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.100
situation like this. You make a great point.&nbsp;
There's nothing you can do to some degree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:36.420
when you are watching the captain of your ship&nbsp;
operate, are there things he was prepared for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:46.140
protocols or is it make noise and pray?
&gt;&gt; When we were prepping for the dive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.420
the captain put us through orientation and told&nbsp;
us stories about people in that situation when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.819
they panic and immediately they want to go&nbsp;
to the escape hatch which is, of course, a

00:04:58.819 --> 00:04:58.831
[6:36:13 AM]

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:02.280
hatch which is, of course, a death sentence,&nbsp;
because when you panic you're not thinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:09.000
So I knew that when we collided, that's the thing&nbsp;
I didn't want to do. I wanted to make sure nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.520
else in the sub was and I would gang tackle&nbsp;
anyone who even looked at that escape hatch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:26.280
For every problem there's an optimal solution.&nbsp;
I've drummed that into my son's head for every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:33.420
problem there's an optimal solution. It is the&nbsp;
most terrifying thing coming to the conclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:42.000
no, there ain't a solution. There is no&nbsp;
solution, period. There's just no one around, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:48.240
I imagine that at some point in&nbsp;
these five folks on the titan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.700
they came to that realization. You could&nbsp;
have all the experience in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:58.800
but at the end of the day there's only so&nbsp;
much you can do. No amount of technology, no

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.720
No amount of technology, no number&nbsp;
of planes, rovs, sonars, nothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:09.720
nothing can come to your rescue. If you're at the&nbsp;
bottom of the ocean two and a half miles down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:15.960
you don't just go down there and reach in even&nbsp;
with a cable or grappling hook. Pressures are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:23.220
enormous, bone crushing, it's cold down&nbsp;
there. I just feel -- I've been feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:35.460
for the last 72 hours this kinship. It's&nbsp;
utterly dark, no light. You don't want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:44.220
light a candle and use up the oxygen. This&nbsp;
had to have been a catastrophic failure.

